This comment is intended for Question 36.[^1]

Response to File Number S7-2026-25 
Question 36[^1]

The Commission should not treat an electronic address supplied by an affiliate or third party as, by itself, an address the covered recipient has “provided” or “accepted to use” for another covered entity’s securities-law disclosures. An electronic address may be accurate and lawfully shared without the recipient knowing that a different entity will use it to replace paper delivery. We support the distinction reflected in the proposing release’s discussion of third-party-supplied addresses.

The Commission should distinguish a service provider delivering information on behalf of the same covered entity from a separate covered entity seeking to satisfy its own delivery obligation. For the latter case, any exception should require the receiving entity to document the address’s source and its current association with the recipient, listing the categories of information and delivery methods used with clear notice through an established channel, and obtain a documented confirmation from the recipient confirming that the identified address may be used for that entity’s covered information before discontinuing paper such as confirmation through a previously authenticated account or physical mail. Other communication that does not pass that set bar such as a test message, absence of a bounce-back, general privacy notice, or broad terms-of-service amendment should not alone establish that choice. We prefer delivery through the issuer or known intermediary, as direct delivery from an unfamiliar party would create phishing or nonreceipt risk. For proxy solicitations, tender offers, and other circumstances governed by separate delivery rules, the Commission should prefer delivery by the issuer or an intermediary already known to the shareholder when direct delivery by an unfamiliar third party would create phishing, privacy, or nonreceipt risks. If direct third-party delivery is permitted, the communication should prominently identify the sender, the transaction, and the source of the address. A prior affirmative paper election should remain effective unless the recipient changes it with intention and of clear record. Any removal of specific verification of the recipient involved could introduce more distance between the managing entity and the recipient or limit the ability of an owner to make informed and current decisions on their directly held investments. 

The Commission should require records of address provenance, the notice, the recipient’s confirmation, delivery failures, remediation, and any paper election. The recipient should be able to correct the address and obtain or elect paper delivery without charge. A known failed delivery should trigger timely delivery of the affected information through a functioning address or paper, consistent with proposed section 303.102(h). For materials tied to voting, tendering, or another deadline, the Commission should consider whether a second channel or a more specific confirmation is needed before a newly supplied address replaces an established channel. 

Finally, the Commission should address the practical value of paper choice and confirm it in new language. Although the proposal already provides paper without a delivery fee, the release states that a covered entity may, where otherwise lawful and disclosed, restrict or terminate a relationship after a paper election. The Commission should evaluate whether that consequence undermines a meaningful opt-out and should prohibit making a recipients’s continued access to ordinary services conditional on accepting electronic delivery, subject to any narrowly justified exceptions. The Commission should amend proposed section 303.102(b)(1)(i) and (f) to provide that a covered entity may not restrict or terminate an otherwise available account or ordinary service solely because a recipient requests paper or opts out of electronic delivery. Any exception should be narrowly defined and supported by a demonstrated operational necessity. Disclosure that termination may occur is not an adequate substitute for a meaningful delivery choice.[^2] We do not ask the Commission to ban legitimate operational sharing of contact information; we ask it not to equate that sharing with proof that legally required information was effectively delivered. The exception for paper delivery discontinuation permitting reliance on an address obtained from an affiliate or third party should apply only when the receiving entity can demonstrate that the recipient was informed through a reliable channel and had an effective, documented means to preserve paper delivery[^3]. Recipients must be able to retain complete trust and confidence that material information will reliably be delivered to them. 

The relevant standard should be effective delivery. An investor should not lose access to required information because an entity relied on contact information obtained from another party without confirming that the investor controls the address and understands how it will be used.  There must be a record of investors being provided an opportunity to review the information and to opt out, and covered entities must utilize an established channel for communication for a given recipient. This careful approach will take additional time but it will lay a strong foundation for the industry to move forward and scale adequately. What must be avoided is the possibility that a large number of recipients are lost in this transition - covered entities must be diligent in tracking their outreach, and in maintaining a record of that outreach. Covered entities should retain auditable records of the address’s source, the notice and delivery method used, the recipient’s confirmation or paper election, and any failed-delivery remediation. Those records should be produced to the Commission upon lawful request. Each recipient should have ready access to records concerning that recipient’s own electronic addresses, delivery preferences, notices, confirmations, opt-out requests, and preference changes.

